WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
On the Home screen, click Open project… and choose the project of the third tutorial.

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.000
The project opens up to date: nothing is to be computed.

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.000
Click Post.

4
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:17.000
Post shows the whole field of the third tutorial. Before any gesture, look at what is left to finish.

5
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:24.000
A veil still lies on the sky: it measures 33 to 34 on a 0 to 255 scale, and the aim is about 26. The faint edges of the ring do not stand out from it. Dehaze will lift it.

6
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.000
The ring is pink and magenta, a little pale. Saturation will make it more vivid, without pushing it towards red.

7
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.000
At full size, the fine structure of the gas, the folds and the edges of the dark globules, is a little soft. Multiscale sharpening will bring it out.

8
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:44.000
What is right stays as it is: the neutral sky, the denoised gas, the round stars and the repaired stain. No colour balance and no noise reduction are needed.

9
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.000
Click the Starless image row: every filter of this tutorial goes there.

10
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Click Filter, then Contrast, then Dehaze.

11
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.000
Drag Amount to the right until the veil lifts without darkening the ring, here 0.25.

12
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.000
Drag Scale to the right, to 256 px or the nearest value the slider gives: here 257 px.

13
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:02.000
The sky is deeper, but the ring has also lost some of its glow: Dehaze acts everywhere on the picture.

14
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.000
Click Add mask.

15
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.000
Click Add to mask, then Luminance range.

16
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.000
Drag To to about 25 %, with From at 0 %: the range selects the sky and the faint tones, not the ring.

17
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.000
Drag Feather to the right, to 8 px, so that the edge of the mask fades.

18
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:20.000
The red tint covers what the mask hides: the ring. Dehaze now acts only on the sky and the faint tones.

19
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:26.000
At 100 %, the faint edges of the ring stand out from a darker sky, and the ring keeps its glow.

20
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.000
Click Filter, then Color, then Saturation.

21
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:32.000
Drag Strength to the right until the ring is more vivid but still pink and magenta, here 20 %.

22
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:38.000
At 100 %, the red and the pink of the ring are more vivid, and the sky stays a neutral grey.

23
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.000
Click Filter, then Detail, then Multiscale sharpening.

24
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.000
Drag the 4 px scale to 0.3.

25
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.000
Drag the 8 px scale to 0.4.

26
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Drag the 16 px scale to 0.3, and leave 1 px and 2 px at 0.

27
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:58.000
At 100 %, the folds and the edges of the globules are crisper. On the whole picture the change is slight: this part is for the full-size picture, a print or a viewer who zooms in.

28
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.000
Click Filter, then Noise.

29
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:08.000
Multiscale noise reduction and Noise reduction stay unused: Denoise already did their work, and one noise reduction is enough.

30
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.000
Click Export…

31
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.000
Choose JPEG in Format.

32
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.000
Click Export.

33
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:23.000
The image is written, with its processing history beside it.

34
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:27.000
The image is exported. Press Ctrl+S to keep the project.
